  "query_preview": "..\n\nAn upper trough continues to sit over southern California and\nnorthern Baja today, while a distinct embedded mid-level \nshortwave was evident this morning on water vapor imagery\nrotating into northern Sonora. This wave will lift\nnorth/northeast through the day today and should produce at \nleast a low end chance of showers and thunderstorms. Though the\nmorning is starting off with widespread cloud cover, models \nshow some clearing likely during the afternoon which should\nallow for some isolated to scattered thunderstorms to form, \nespecially off higher terrain. High- resolution models have been\nvery inconsistent in this potential however and the exiting \nwave may produce enough subsidence to keep coverage on the low \nside, so confidence in type of coverage either way is low. With\nprecipitable water of 1-1.4 inches common across the area, a \nflash flooding threat may accompany any thunderstorm. \n\nPrecipitable water will drop below an inch behind the exiting \nwave on Friday which will heavily reduce shower/thunderstorm\nchances. High terrain build-ups with a few brief showers and \nthunderstorms in the afternoon can't be ruled out however as \nlow level moisture should linger.\n\nTransient ridging on Saturday will keep the forecast dry as well\nas warm temperatures up a few degrees above normal. The dry\nforecast is short lived as a Pacific trough approaches and then\ncrosses central to southern California Sunday and Monday. The \npattern shift will bring moisture back into southern Arizona, \nthough there remains discrepancies on when exactly \nprecipitation returns with it from Sunday to Monday. Mainly \n10-20 percent chances are forecast on Sunday with increased \nchances Monday into Tuesday as richer moisture will likely \narrive, at least partially aided by Hurricane Polo to the south.\nBy Monday/Tuesday Hurricane Polo is forecast by the NHC to be \noff the southwest Baja Peninsula coast, with high uncertainty \nregarding its track after this point. For now the forecast dries\nout Wednesday and Thursday given the uncertainty and the \nlikelihood of the exiting trough clearing moisture out of the \nregion. Temperatures during this period may also see a \nnoticeable swing, with the potential for well below normal \ntemperatures Tuesday and Wednesday if the trough is on the \ndeeper end of the spectrum of reasonable possibilities.",
